      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\), we start by expressing the radicand in terms of powers of 3. Note that \\(27\\) can be written as \\(3^3\\) and \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) can be written as \\(3^{1/2}\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3} = \\log_3 (3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2})\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the properties of exponents to combine the terms inside the logarithm:\n\n\\[\n3^3 \\cdot 3^{1/2} = 3^{3 + 1/2} = 3^{6/2 + 1/2} = 3^{7/2}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^{7/2})\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\log_b (b^x) = x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^{7/2}) = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\log_3 27\\sqrt{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{7}{2}\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
